  "hints": [
    "Cancelling the fewest is the same as keeping the most non-overlapping slots.",
    "Among all slots that could be kept first, which one leaves the most room for the rest?",
    "Sort by end time and keep a slot whenever its start is at least the end of the last kept slot. The answer is n minus the number kept."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function fewestRemovals(slots) {\n  const sorted = [...slots].sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1])\n  let kept = 0\n  let lastEnd = -Infinity\n  for (const [start, end] of sorted) {\n    if (start >= lastEnd) {\n      kept++\n      lastEnd = end\n    }\n  }\n  return slots.length - kept\n}\n",
    "py": "def fewest_removals(slots):\n    kept = 0\n    last_end = float(\"-inf\")\n    for start, end in sorted(slots, key=lambda slot: slot[1]):\n        if start >= last_end:\n            kept += 1\n            last_end = end\n    return len(slots) - kept\n",
    "explanation": "Keeping the slot that ends earliest is always safe: in any optimal selection you can swap its first slot for this one without creating an overlap. Repeating that choice on the slots that start after it gives a maximum set of compatible slots, and everything else must be cancelled."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "notes": "Sorting dominates; the sweep is linear. Sorting by start time instead is a common bug unless you also keep the smaller end on conflicts."
  },
